1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:03,660
How many volts are there across
the two terminals of an open switch?

2
00:00:03,890 --> 00:00:05,790
The first answer
that comes to mind is zero.

3
00:00:05,870 --> 00:00:08,130
After all, no current
flows through the switch.

4
00:00:08,210 --> 00:00:10,228
But in the circuit we're about to draw,

5
00:00:10,308 --> 00:00:13,930
there are exactly 9 V across
the terminals of the open switch.

6
00:00:14,010 --> 00:00:16,370
The current is zero,
but the voltage is not.

7
00:00:16,450 --> 00:00:20,660
And the most common mistake on this
topic is exactly confusing these two.

8
00:00:20,820 --> 00:00:24,210
By the end of this part, you'll find
the voltage across an open switch.

9
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:26,144
You'll write current
with its direction and find

10
00:00:26,224 --> 00:00:28,180
the charge that passed
from a current graph.

11
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:30,630
You'll build a circuit
that delivers a required current.

12
00:00:30,710 --> 00:00:34,140
In a datasheet, you'll tell continuous
current apart from pulse current,

13
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:37,490
and you'll calculate a battery's
life from the average current.

14
00:00:47,150 --> 00:00:51,070
Hello everyone, welcome
to the Circuit Theory I series.

15
00:00:51,150 --> 00:00:55,115
Today, in the second part of Lecture 1,
we'll talk about the direction of current,

16
00:00:55,195 --> 00:00:58,080
the open circuit,
and going from current to charge.

17
00:00:58,450 --> 00:01:00,295
In Part 1, we defined current as the rate

18
00:01:00,375 --> 00:01:02,920
at which charge passes
through a cross-section.

19
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:04,386
Before we get into the topic,

20
00:01:04,466 --> 00:01:06,586
let's see what the average
and instantaneous

21
00:01:06,666 --> 00:01:09,510
current from there correspond
to in a real design.

22
00:01:10,150 --> 00:01:12,240
Open a component's datasheet.

23
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:14,349
On this page from the manufacturer,

24
00:01:14,429 --> 00:01:17,910
you'll often see not just
one row for current, but two.

25
00:01:17,990 --> 00:01:19,430
The first is the continuous current:

26
00:01:19,510 --> 00:01:22,070
the current the component can
withstand for a long time.

27
00:01:22,150 --> 00:01:23,616
The second is the pulse current:

28
00:01:23,696 --> 00:01:26,640
the current it can withstand
only for a very short time.

29
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:29,270
Let's look at a widely
used rectifier diode.

30
00:01:29,350 --> 00:01:32,950
The datasheet lists an average
forward current of 1 A.

31
00:01:33,030 --> 00:01:36,680
This 1 A is given at an ambient
temperature of 75 °C.

32
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:40,190
The name of this row explicitly
includes the word average.

33
00:01:40,270 --> 00:01:44,578
Right next to it, the surge
current is listed as 30 A,

34
00:01:44,658 --> 00:01:47,700
but only for a single 8.3 ms pulse.

35
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:50,215
So this diode can
withstand a brief stress,

36
00:01:50,295 --> 00:01:52,740
but it can't carry 30 A continuously.

37
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:56,390
You'll also see two rows
for an electronic switch and for an LED.

38
00:01:56,470 --> 00:01:59,920
But there, the first row isn't called
average; it's called continuous current.

39
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,090
These values are limits that must not
be exceeded, not operating points.

40
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:06,578
The continuous current
is mostly limited by heating,

41
00:02:06,658 --> 00:02:08,770
and it drops as the temperature rises.

42
00:02:08,850 --> 00:02:12,515
For example,
the switch's continuous current

43
00:02:12,595 --> 00:02:16,210
is 260 mA at 25 °C and 190 mA at 85 °C.

44
00:02:16,290 --> 00:02:19,840
So when you read a current row,
ask yourself three questions.

45
00:02:19,930 --> 00:02:22,210
Is this value continuous or pulse?

46
00:02:22,290 --> 00:02:23,910
For what duration is it given?

47
00:02:23,990 --> 00:02:26,820
At what temperature,
and how is it mounted?

48
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:30,630
If you haven't read a value's conditions,
you haven't really read the value.

49
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:32,570
Now let's do a small design.

50
00:02:32,650 --> 00:02:38,650
A temperature sensor wakes up once
every second and draws 10 mA for 2 ms.

51
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:43,410
For the remaining 998 ms,
it sleeps and draws 5 µA.

52
00:02:43,490 --> 00:02:48,210
The coin cell powering
it is labeled 225 mAh.

53
00:02:48,290 --> 00:02:50,630
A milliampere-hour
is actually a unit of charge.

54
00:02:50,710 --> 00:02:52,810
1 hour is 3600 seconds.

55
00:02:52,890 --> 00:02:54,939
1 mA × 3600 s = 3600 mC,

56
00:02:55,019 --> 00:03:01,019
because multiplying milliamperes
by seconds gives you millicoulombs.

57
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:02,900
That is 3.6 C.

58
00:03:02,980 --> 00:03:06,210
So the battery is telling us
the total charge it can deliver.

59
00:03:06,290 --> 00:03:08,260
How much charge is drawn in 1 second?

60
00:03:08,340 --> 00:03:10,910
With a constant current,
charge is current times time.

61
00:03:10,990 --> 00:03:15,220
While awake: 10 mA × 2 ms = 20 µC.

62
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:17,502
If you multiply milliamperes
by milliseconds,

63
00:03:17,582 --> 00:03:19,360
you get microcoulombs directly.

64
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:23,470
While asleep: 5 µA × 998 ms.

65
00:03:23,550 --> 00:03:27,130
5 × 998 = 4990.

66
00:03:27,210 --> 00:03:30,680
Multiplying microamperes
by milliseconds gives you nanocoulombs.

67
00:03:30,760 --> 00:03:33,430
So 4990 nC.

68
00:03:33,510 --> 00:03:38,540
Since 1000 nC = 1 µC, this is 4.99 µC.

69
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:44,110
The total is 20 + 4.99 = 24.99 µC.

70
00:03:44,190 --> 00:03:48,810
Dividing this by 1 second,
the average current is about 25 µA.

71
00:03:49,570 --> 00:03:52,650
The battery life is the capacity
divided by the average current.

72
00:03:52,730 --> 00:03:56,910
25 µA = 0.025 mA.

73
00:03:56,990 --> 00:04:00,230
Dividing milliampere-hours
by milliamperes leaves hours.

74
00:04:00,310 --> 00:04:03,980
Dividing by 0.025 means multiplying by 40.

75
00:04:04,060 --> 00:04:06,970
225 × 40 = 9000 hours.

76
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:11,910
Dividing 9000 by 24 gives 375
days, that is, about a year.

77
00:04:11,990 --> 00:04:15,640
The real life may be somewhat shorter
because of temperature and the pulses,

78
00:04:15,720 --> 00:04:17,500
so the designer leaves a margin.

79
00:04:17,580 --> 00:04:19,870
Another costly mistake in design is this:

80
00:04:19,950 --> 00:04:24,800
seeing that the sensor draws 10 mA and
calculating the life with this peak value.

81
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:28,120
225 / 10 = 22.5 hours.

82
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:29,430
That's not even a day.

83
00:04:29,510 --> 00:04:32,460
9000 / 22.5 = 400.

84
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:35,640
So it's about 400 times shorter
than the correct result.

85
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:38,863
In short, the average current
determines the battery life,

86
00:04:38,943 --> 00:04:42,490
while the pulse row in the datasheet
limits the short peaks.

87
00:04:42,570 --> 00:04:45,620
You can subscribe to the channel
to follow the rest of this series.

88
00:04:46,773 --> 00:04:49,993
Now let's move on to three
important points about current.

89
00:04:50,073 --> 00:04:54,053
The first point: a single number
isn't enough to describe a current.

90
00:04:54,133 --> 00:04:57,113
Every current has a value
and also a direction.

91
00:04:57,193 --> 00:04:59,563
We show the direction
on the schematic with an arrow.

92
00:04:59,643 --> 00:05:01,543
We call this arrow
the reference direction.

93
00:05:01,623 --> 00:05:04,673
We decide which way to draw the arrow.

94
00:05:04,753 --> 00:05:06,676
Our rule is this: positive charge passing

95
00:05:06,756 --> 00:05:09,323
in the direction of the arrow
counts as positive.

96
00:05:09,403 --> 00:05:12,643
Positive charge passing against
the arrow counts as negative.

97
00:05:12,723 --> 00:05:13,823
Let's look at an example.

98
00:05:13,903 --> 00:05:19,243
In a wire, 3 µC of positive charge passes
from left to right every millisecond.

99
00:05:19,323 --> 00:05:22,723
3 µC / 1 ms = 3 mA.

100
00:05:22,803 --> 00:05:26,093
If we draw the arrow to the right,
the current is +3 mA.

101
00:05:26,173 --> 00:05:27,902
If we draw the arrow to the left,

102
00:05:27,982 --> 00:05:31,433
the charge goes against the arrow,
and the current is −3 mA.

103
00:05:31,513 --> 00:05:32,863
So which one is correct?

104
00:05:32,943 --> 00:05:36,363
Both. Both answers describe
the same physical situation.

105
00:05:36,443 --> 00:05:39,383
The minus sign doesn't mean
you chose the wrong direction.

106
00:05:39,463 --> 00:05:42,253
It just means the charge
is moving against the arrow.

107
00:05:42,333 --> 00:05:44,833
As long as the arrow
is drawn on the schematic.

108
00:05:44,913 --> 00:05:46,323
So what actually happens?

109
00:05:46,403 --> 00:05:49,339
In a metal wire,
what actually moves are electrons,

110
00:05:49,419 --> 00:05:51,823
and the electron's charge is negative.

111
00:05:51,903 --> 00:05:54,504
Suppose the electrons move to the right

112
00:05:54,584 --> 00:05:57,943
and carry −4 µC to the right
every millisecond.

113
00:05:58,023 --> 00:05:59,103
Let's draw the arrow to the right.

114
00:05:59,183 --> 00:06:03,263
−4 µC / 1 ms = −4 mA.

115
00:06:03,343 --> 00:06:06,153
If we flip the arrow to the left, +4 mA.

116
00:06:06,233 --> 00:06:11,453
So electrons moving to the right
mean a current of 4 mA to the left.

117
00:06:11,533 --> 00:06:12,816
The direction of current is defined

118
00:06:12,896 --> 00:06:14,893
as the direction in which
positive charge moves.

119
00:06:14,973 --> 00:06:17,913
That's why it's opposite
to the motion of electrons.

120
00:06:17,993 --> 00:06:22,383
The −2 mA we found in the last
example of Part 1 was exactly this.

121
00:06:22,463 --> 00:06:27,263
At 4 seconds, 6 − 2 × 4 = −2 mA.

122
00:06:27,343 --> 00:06:29,236
After 3 seconds,
the charge had started passing

123
00:06:29,316 --> 00:06:31,653
through the cross-section
in the opposite direction.

124
00:06:31,733 --> 00:06:35,103
In short, draw an arrow next
to every current on the schematic.

125
00:06:35,183 --> 00:06:38,513
A current value without an arrow
is incomplete information.

126
00:06:39,033 --> 00:06:41,053
The second point is the open circuit.

127
00:06:41,133 --> 00:06:42,723
Now let's build this circuit.

128
00:06:42,803 --> 00:06:46,166
A 9 V battery, a 3 kΩ
resistor and a switch

129
00:06:46,246 --> 00:06:49,383
are connected to form a single loop.

130
00:06:49,463 --> 00:06:51,173
First, let the switch be closed.

131
00:06:51,383 --> 00:06:54,763
A closed ideal switch
is like a piece of wire.

132
00:06:54,843 --> 00:06:58,433
The battery's 9 V appears
directly across the resistor.

133
00:06:58,513 --> 00:07:01,633
By Ohm's law, the current
is voltage divided by resistance.

134
00:07:01,713 --> 00:07:04,663
9 V / 3 kΩ = 3 mA.

135
00:07:04,743 --> 00:07:07,163
V / kΩ gives mA.

136
00:07:07,363 --> 00:07:08,843
Now let's open the switch.

137
00:07:08,923 --> 00:07:11,143
An open switch is an open circuit.

138
00:07:11,223 --> 00:07:14,203
There's no path for charge
to pass between its two terminals.

139
00:07:14,283 --> 00:07:16,203
If there's no path, there's no current.

140
00:07:16,293 --> 00:07:17,975
Since there's only one path in the loop,

141
00:07:18,055 --> 00:07:20,303
the current is zero
everywhere in the loop.

142
00:07:20,453 --> 00:07:23,623
So what's the voltage
across the open switch?

143
00:07:23,703 --> 00:07:26,343
This is where the most
common mistake is made.

144
00:07:26,423 --> 00:07:28,053
Saying that since the current is zero

145
00:07:28,133 --> 00:07:30,603
the voltage must be zero too,
and writing 0 V.

146
00:07:30,693 --> 00:07:33,633
The correct way is this.
No current flows through the resistor.

147
00:07:33,713 --> 00:07:39,683
By Ohm's law, the resistor's voltage
is 3 kΩ × 0 mA, that is, 0 V.

148
00:07:39,763 --> 00:07:42,803
That means both ends of the resistor
are at the same voltage.

149
00:07:42,883 --> 00:07:44,505
The switch's lower terminal is connected

150
00:07:44,585 --> 00:07:46,693
to the battery's negative
terminal by a wire.

151
00:07:46,773 --> 00:07:49,617
Its upper terminal is connected
to the battery's positive terminal

152
00:07:49,697 --> 00:07:53,133
through the resistor, and the resistor
adds no voltage difference at all.

153
00:07:53,213 --> 00:07:57,873
So the battery's full 9 V remains
across the terminals of the switch.

154
00:07:57,953 --> 00:08:00,853
You'll see this on almost
every electronic board.

155
00:08:00,933 --> 00:08:06,423
A microcontroller's input is connected
to 3.3 V through a 10 kΩ resistor.

156
00:08:06,503 --> 00:08:08,833
The button is between the input
and ground.

157
00:08:08,913 --> 00:08:11,703
The input itself draws almost no current.

158
00:08:11,783 --> 00:08:13,478
When the button is open,
it's an open circuit,

159
00:08:13,558 --> 00:08:15,483
and no current flows through the resistor.

160
00:08:15,563 --> 00:08:19,333
The resistor's voltage is 10 kΩ × 0 = 0 V.

161
00:08:19,413 --> 00:08:24,973
So the input is at 3.3 V, and there are
also 3.3 V across the button's terminals.

162
00:08:25,053 --> 00:08:31,053
When you press the button, the current
becomes 3.3 V / 10 kΩ = 0.33 mA.

163
00:08:31,323 --> 00:08:35,203
A closed button is like a wire;
it connects the input directly to ground.

164
00:08:35,283 --> 00:08:37,533
That's why the input drops to 0 V.

165
00:08:37,643 --> 00:08:43,023
So an open circuit guarantees us only one
thing: the current through it is zero.

166
00:08:43,103 --> 00:08:47,083
On its own, it says nothing about
the voltage across its terminals.

167
00:08:47,163 --> 00:08:50,663
That voltage comes from the elements
connected to the open circuit.

168
00:08:51,503 --> 00:08:54,703
The third point is the reverse
of what we did in Part 1.

169
00:08:54,783 --> 00:08:56,301
There, we went from charge to current:

170
00:08:56,381 --> 00:08:58,923
we took the derivative
of charge with respect to time.

171
00:08:59,003 --> 00:09:00,773
Now we have the current.

172
00:09:00,853 --> 00:09:03,983
How do we find how much
charge passed in a given time?

173
00:09:04,143 --> 00:09:06,133
Think of a tiny time interval.

174
00:09:06,213 --> 00:09:09,193
dt is the name of this
very short time interval.

175
00:09:09,273 --> 00:09:12,513
In such a short time,
the current stays almost constant.

176
00:09:12,593 --> 00:09:16,123
The charge passing
in that interval = i × dt.

177
00:09:16,203 --> 00:09:18,383
On the graph, this is a thin strip.

178
00:09:18,463 --> 00:09:19,663
Its height is i.

179
00:09:19,743 --> 00:09:22,543
And its width is that tiny time. dt.

180
00:09:22,623 --> 00:09:25,842
If we add up all these strips
from time zero to time t₁,

181
00:09:25,922 --> 00:09:28,443
we find the total charge that passed.

182
00:09:28,523 --> 00:09:30,323
The name of this sum is the integral.

183
00:09:30,403 --> 00:09:35,493
q = ∫₀^t₁ i dt.

184
00:09:35,573 --> 00:09:37,463
Its meaning on the graph is very clear.

185
00:09:37,543 --> 00:09:40,873
The charge that passed is the area
under the current graph.

186
00:09:40,953 --> 00:09:44,333
Remember: the charges we found
as current times time in the battery

187
00:09:44,413 --> 00:09:47,473
calculation were actually
areas of narrow rectangles too.

188
00:09:47,553 --> 00:09:51,123
And the battery's milliampere-hours are
the largest area the battery can deliver.

189
00:09:51,203 --> 00:09:52,603
Let's try the simplest case.

190
00:09:52,683 --> 00:09:56,353
A constant current of 4 mA
flows for 3 seconds.

191
00:09:56,433 --> 00:09:57,823
The graph is a rectangle.

192
00:09:57,903 --> 00:10:01,043
Its height is 4 mA,
and its width is 3 seconds.

193
00:10:01,123 --> 00:10:03,243
The area is height times width.

194
00:10:03,323 --> 00:10:04,993
4 × 3 = 12.

195
00:10:05,073 --> 00:10:07,803
mA × s gives mC.

196
00:10:07,883 --> 00:10:10,933
So 12 mC has passed in 3 seconds.

197
00:10:11,013 --> 00:10:13,603
Let's also test this method
with the example from Part 1.

198
00:10:13,683 --> 00:10:15,371
There, the charge was 2t² mC,

199
00:10:15,451 --> 00:10:19,393
and by taking its derivative we
found the current to be 4t mA.

200
00:10:19,473 --> 00:10:20,823
Now let's go the other way.

201
00:10:20,903 --> 00:10:26,723
The integral of 4t is half
of 4 times t², that is, 2t².

202
00:10:26,803 --> 00:10:30,664
If we integrate from 0 to 3 seconds,

203
00:10:30,744 --> 00:10:33,323
2 × 3² = 2 × 9 = 18 mC.

204
00:10:33,403 --> 00:10:36,653
The very same 18 mC we found in Part 1.

205
00:10:36,733 --> 00:10:39,213
Derivative and integral are
the inverse of each other.

206
00:10:39,293 --> 00:10:40,913
There's also the matter of sign.

207
00:10:40,993 --> 00:10:44,763
If the current is negative, the graph's
curve goes below the horizontal axis.

208
00:10:44,843 --> 00:10:47,123
The area below the axis
counts as negative.

209
00:10:47,203 --> 00:10:50,453
Because during that time, the charge
passes against the reference arrow.

210
00:10:50,533 --> 00:10:55,463
For example, if a current
of −2 mA flows for 2 seconds,

211
00:10:55,543 --> 00:10:57,183
2 × (−2) = −4 mC.

212
00:10:57,263 --> 00:11:00,973
That means 4 mC of charge
has passed against the arrow.

213
00:11:01,053 --> 00:11:04,063
Also remember the unit shortcut
we saw in the battery calculation:

214
00:11:04,143 --> 00:11:06,184
if you multiply milliamperes
by milliseconds,

215
00:11:06,264 --> 00:11:07,793
you get microcoulombs directly.

216
00:11:07,873 --> 00:11:10,263
We'll use it again right now.

217
00:11:10,783 --> 00:11:13,113
Now a more interesting current graph.

218
00:11:13,193 --> 00:11:16,752
The current starts from zero at time zero

219
00:11:16,832 --> 00:11:20,143
and rises linearly to 6 mA in 2 ms.

220
00:11:20,223 --> 00:11:24,383
Between 2 and 5 ms,
it stays constant at 6 mA.

221
00:11:24,463 --> 00:11:30,193
At 5 ms, it changes direction
and is −2 mA until 8 ms.

222
00:11:30,273 --> 00:11:32,293
The question: between 0 and 8 ms,

223
00:11:32,373 --> 00:11:36,223
how much net charge passed
in the direction of the arrow?

224
00:11:36,303 --> 00:11:37,743
Let's split the area into pieces.

225
00:11:37,823 --> 00:11:39,243
The first piece is a triangle.

226
00:11:39,323 --> 00:11:42,533
The area of a triangle
is base × height / 2.

227
00:11:42,613 --> 00:11:46,063
The base is 2 ms, and the height is 6 mA.

228
00:11:46,143 --> 00:11:49,523
2 × 6 = 12, and half of 12 is 6.

229
00:11:49,603 --> 00:11:53,263
So 6 µC has passed in the first 2 ms.

230
00:11:53,343 --> 00:11:55,063
The second piece is a rectangle.

231
00:11:55,143 --> 00:11:58,073
Its width is 5 − 2 = 3 ms.

232
00:11:58,153 --> 00:12:00,043
Its height is 6 mA.

233
00:12:00,123 --> 00:12:02,703
3 × 6 = 18 µC.

234
00:12:02,783 --> 00:12:06,633
The third piece is a rectangle again,
but this time below the axis.

235
00:12:06,713 --> 00:12:09,663
Its width is 8 − 5 = 3 ms.

236
00:12:09,743 --> 00:12:11,903
Its height is −2 mA.

237
00:12:11,983 --> 00:12:14,903
3 × (−2) = −6 µC.

238
00:12:14,983 --> 00:12:17,493
During this time, the charge
passes against the arrow.

239
00:12:17,573 --> 00:12:18,593
Now let's add them up.

240
00:12:18,673 --> 00:12:20,643
6 + 18 = 24.

241
00:12:20,723 --> 00:12:24,033
Subtracting 6 from 24 gives 18 µC.

242
00:12:24,893 --> 00:12:27,682
Remember Part 1: if you divide
the net charge by the time,

243
00:12:27,762 --> 00:12:29,293
you get the average current.

244
00:12:29,373 --> 00:12:34,103
18 µC / 8 ms = 2.25 mA.

245
00:12:34,183 --> 00:12:37,511
Look at the graph: the current doesn't
stay at this value in any interval;

246
00:12:37,591 --> 00:12:40,513
it passes through this value
only for an instant on the ramp.

247
00:12:40,593 --> 00:12:42,503
Once again, this is only an average.

248
00:12:42,583 --> 00:12:44,263
And this average
is exactly what was useful

249
00:12:44,343 --> 00:12:46,093
to us in the battery life calculation.

250
00:12:46,173 --> 00:12:47,173
Let's also look at this.

251
00:12:47,253 --> 00:12:51,403
At 1 ms, right in the middle of the ramp,
how much charge has passed?

252
00:12:51,483 --> 00:12:55,403
The current rises by 6 mA in 2 ms.

253
00:12:55,483 --> 00:12:58,743
6 / 2, that's 3 mA every millisecond.

254
00:12:58,823 --> 00:13:01,963
At that instant,
the current is 3 × 1 = 3 mA.

255
00:13:02,043 --> 00:13:06,603
The area of the small triangle
is 1 × 3 / 2 = 1.5 µC.

256
00:13:06,683 --> 00:13:09,803
Is this half of the 6 µC
in the first triangle?

257
00:13:09,883 --> 00:13:14,173
No. Dividing 1.5 by 6 gives 0.25.

258
00:13:14,253 --> 00:13:15,393
So it's a quarter.

259
00:13:15,473 --> 00:13:16,653
Why a quarter?

260
00:13:16,733 --> 00:13:19,813
Because in the first half of the ramp,
the current is still small.

261
00:13:19,893 --> 00:13:23,003
Most of the charge passes in the second
half, when the current has grown.

262
00:13:23,083 --> 00:13:26,023
Half the time doesn't
mean half the charge.

263
00:13:26,607 --> 00:13:28,767
Finally, let's turn the problem around.

264
00:13:28,847 --> 00:13:31,487
This time, we're given a current waveform.

265
00:13:31,567 --> 00:13:34,757
And we're asked to build a circuit
that produces this current.

266
00:13:34,837 --> 00:13:36,367
The current we want is like this.

267
00:13:36,447 --> 00:13:38,217
Zero until 1 second.

268
00:13:38,297 --> 00:13:41,247
2 mA between 1 and 3 seconds.

269
00:13:41,327 --> 00:13:44,327
6 mA between 3 and 4 seconds.

270
00:13:44,407 --> 00:13:46,687
Zero again after 4 seconds.

271
00:13:46,767 --> 00:13:48,837
This current will be drawn from a battery.

272
00:13:48,917 --> 00:13:53,257
You have a 12 V battery,
two resistors and two switches.

273
00:13:53,337 --> 00:13:56,447
Pause for a moment and think:
how would you build this circuit?

274
00:13:57,937 --> 00:13:58,957
Here's one solution.

275
00:13:59,037 --> 00:14:02,447
We connect two separate branches across
the two terminals of the battery.

276
00:14:02,547 --> 00:14:06,667
The first branch has the first
switch and a 6 kΩ resistor.

277
00:14:06,747 --> 00:14:10,777
The second branch has the second
switch and a 2 kΩ resistor.

278
00:14:10,857 --> 00:14:13,727
The two switches are never
closed at the same time.

279
00:14:13,807 --> 00:14:16,077
So where did we get these
resistor values from?

280
00:14:16,157 --> 00:14:18,247
We used Ohm's law in reverse.

281
00:14:18,327 --> 00:14:20,117
Resistance is voltage divided by current.

282
00:14:20,197 --> 00:14:24,847
If we want 2 mA, 12 V / 2 mA = 6 kΩ.

283
00:14:24,927 --> 00:14:29,717
If we want 6 mA, 12 V / 6 mA = 2 kΩ.

284
00:14:29,797 --> 00:14:32,217
V / mA gives kΩ.

285
00:14:32,297 --> 00:14:33,967
Now let's follow it step by step.

286
00:14:34,047 --> 00:14:36,787
Until 1 second, both switches are open.

287
00:14:36,867 --> 00:14:39,877
An open circuit guarantees
that the current is zero.

288
00:14:39,957 --> 00:14:41,687
No current is drawn from the battery.

289
00:14:41,767 --> 00:14:44,737
At 1 second, the first switch closes.

290
00:14:44,817 --> 00:14:48,847
The battery's 12 V appears
across the 6 kΩ resistor.

291
00:14:48,927 --> 00:14:53,927
By Ohm's law, the current
is 12 V / 6 kΩ = 2 mA.

292
00:14:54,007 --> 00:14:56,647
The second branch is open,
so no current flows through it.

293
00:14:56,727 --> 00:14:59,597
So the current drawn
from the battery is 2 mA.

294
00:14:59,677 --> 00:15:01,819
At 3 seconds, the first switch opens,

295
00:15:01,899 --> 00:15:04,957
and at the same moment
the second switch closes.

296
00:15:05,037 --> 00:15:08,267
Now the 12 V is across the 2 kΩ resistor.

297
00:15:08,347 --> 00:15:11,207
The current is 12 / 2 = 6 mA.

298
00:15:11,287 --> 00:15:14,377
At 4 seconds, the second switch opens too.

299
00:15:14,457 --> 00:15:15,857
The current is zero again.

300
00:15:15,937 --> 00:15:19,076
But notice: since no current
flows through either resistor,

301
00:15:19,156 --> 00:15:22,197
there are 12 V across each
of the two open switches.

302
00:15:22,277 --> 00:15:25,827
Just like the 9 V circuit
in the open circuit example.

303
00:15:25,907 --> 00:15:29,447
Finally, let's find the total charge
carried by this current from the area.

304
00:15:29,527 --> 00:15:32,557
The first rectangle: 3 − 1 = 2 seconds.

305
00:15:32,637 --> 00:15:36,427
2 s × 2 mA = 4 mC.

306
00:15:36,507 --> 00:15:39,147
The second rectangle: 4 − 3 = 1 second.

307
00:15:39,227 --> 00:15:42,647
1 s × 6 mA = 6 mC.

308
00:15:42,727 --> 00:15:45,877
The total is 4 + 6 = 10 mC.

309
00:15:46,843 --> 00:15:50,633
Let's sum up. Every current has
a value and also a direction.

310
00:15:50,763 --> 00:15:54,343
An open circuit guarantees
only that the current is zero.

311
00:15:54,423 --> 00:15:57,523
The voltage across its terminals
is determined by the surrounding circuit.

312
00:15:57,623 --> 00:16:00,323
To go from current to charge,
we take the integral.

313
00:16:00,403 --> 00:16:03,239
The charge that passed is the area
under the current graph,

314
00:16:03,319 --> 00:16:05,993
and the area below the axis
counts as negative.

315
00:16:06,073 --> 00:16:08,987
We also came across these two
quantities in the datasheet:

316
00:16:09,067 --> 00:16:11,472
the average current
determines the battery life,

317
00:16:11,552 --> 00:16:14,793
while the pulse current sets
the limit for brief stress.

318
00:16:14,973 --> 00:16:16,333
Let me leave you with a question too.

319
00:16:16,413 --> 00:16:20,244
A current of 5 mA flows
through a wire for 2 seconds,

320
00:16:20,324 --> 00:16:23,273
and then −3 mA for the next 1 second.

321
00:16:23,353 --> 00:16:25,361
At the end of 3 seconds,
how much net charge

322
00:16:25,441 --> 00:16:27,623
has passed in the direction of the arrow?

323
00:16:27,703 --> 00:16:29,203
Write your answer in the comments.

324
00:16:29,403 --> 00:16:30,647
If this lecture helped you,

325
00:16:30,727 --> 00:16:33,623
please like the video
and share it with your classmates.

326
00:16:33,703 --> 00:16:36,543
If you subscribe to the channel,
you won't miss Part 3.

327
00:16:36,623 --> 00:16:39,063
In Part 3, we'll see what voltage is,

328
00:16:39,143 --> 00:16:43,323
what a short circuit guarantees us,
and the ideal source.

